WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:01.648
Hi.

2
00:00:01.648 --> 00:00:02.749
Hi guys.
Good morning

3
00:00:02.749 --> 00:00:04.000
That's a great outfit.

4
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:04.833
Oh!

5
00:00:04.833 --> 00:00:05.666
You look great.

6
00:00:05.666 --> 00:00:07.580
Well, thank you, Folake.

7
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:09.253
I really appreciate that.

8
00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:12.570
How are you guys liking,

9
00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:15.090
you know, being on this hit show

10
00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:17.310
about something so meaningful

11
00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:19.340
love and, you know

12
00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:21.400
it feels so timeless,

13
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.780
but also so modern in the themes

14
00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:26.550
that you guys are discussing on the show.

15
00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:27.590
I'm so proud of this show.

16
00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:29.670
I had one show with "Mike and Molly,"

17
00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:31.050
and that was a gift

18
00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:32.070
and life-changing.

19
00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:34.830
And this one just feels right,

20
00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:36.360
because without preaching,

21
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.830 line:15%
it's just a great example %of love.

22
00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:41.770 line:15%
And it's an example of,

23
00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:43.830 line:15%
if you're luc%ky enough to find that person

24
00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:46.730
to walk through this life with then you win.

25
00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:48.160
That's what the world is about.

26
00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:49.060
If at the end of the day,

27
00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:51.570
you can reach over and there's a hand to hold,

28
00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:52.711
you're ahead of the game.

29
00:00:52.711 --> 00:00:54.730
And I love how different we are

30
00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:56.010
because it does

31
00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:58.300
you know, the more we find out about our differences

32
00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.480
the more we find out we are the same.

33
00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.490
And there's just a lovely connection.

34
00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:05.450
And what's fun about becoming a couple is,

35
00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:07.060
and I think where a lot of comedy is,

36
00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:08.660
you have to blend the families.

37
00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:12.200
And we all know that's not an easy ride sometimes.

38
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:13.960
And I think that makes for good humor.

39
00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:16.760
And I think it's identifiable to everybody

40
00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:21.760
Folake, do you think that the show really brings home

41
00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:23.470
the opposites attract,

42
00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:26.600
and you find this common ground which,

43
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:30.200
my goodness, is so thematic of our world.

44
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.360
Yeah, I like to think of it

45
00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:36.680
as sort of like an aspirational show.

46
00:01:38.367 --> 00:01:40.380 line:15%
It's a good reminder that,

47
00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:41.960 line:15%
it doesn't matter where you come from,

48
00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:43.880 line:15%
it doesn't matter what you look like.

49
00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:44.713 line:15%
At the end of the day,

50
00:01:44.713 --> 00:01:46.350 line:15%
we all want to be surrounded by love.

51
00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:49.700
We all want to be able to provide for our loved ones.

52
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:52.350
And that is the most important thing.

53
00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:54.050
I think it's very timely

54
00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:59.050
during this past few years of anti-immigration sentiment.

55
00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.610
And just to remind people that,

56
00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:04.470
like immigrants are human beings

57
00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:07.610
and we're coming here to look for a better life.

58
00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:12.460
Absolutely, I couldn't agree more.

59
00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:16.230
I do love though you were born in Nigeria.

60
00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:17.063
Yes.

61
00:02:17.063 --> 00:02:19.250
And something that you said really spoke to me

62
00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:20.380
and made me giggle,

63
00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:23.050
because you said in Nigeria,

64
00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:24.780
people just say it like it is.

65
00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:27.363
And in America we have to put a smile on it.

66
00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:29.603
Yeah.

67
00:02:30.853 --> 00:02:33.520
(laughs loudly)

68
00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:34.570
Like in Nigeria,

69
00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:36.670
you have to understand the climate there.

70
00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:37.990
It's really hot.

71
00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:40.370
It's really fast-paced in Lagos where I grew up.

72
00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:42.330 line:15%
It's really fast-paced.

73
00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:44.330 line:15%
The government doesn't necessarily look out for you

74
00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:46.730 line:15%
so you're out there building the roads yourself,

75
00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:50.010 line:15%
connecting your electric power lines yourself.

76
00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:54.140
Like, you have to be really, really straight to the point

77
00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:56.960
and communicate as efficiently

78
00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:58.680
and effectively as possible.

79
00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:01.400
So whenever I need like a critique

80
00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:02.900
or feedback on something,

81
00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:05.540
I just call my brothers at home and they're like,

82
00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:07.320
no, that's stupid.

83
00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:08.403
Yeah, that's good.

84
00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:10.610
And they get straight to the point

85
00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:13.360
we have a conversation in like less than three minutes.

86
00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.840
You know, I'm someone who flowers a lot of things up.

87
00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:19.250
I'm very long-winded,

88
00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:21.160
I don't know how to edit.

89
00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:22.650
I have so much to learn every day.

90
00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:23.754
I'm just a student.

91
00:03:23.754 --> 00:03:24.587
If you couldn't be offended,

92
00:03:24.587 --> 00:03:25.740
we can totally work on it.

93
00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:26.620
Alright, good.

94
00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:30.420
Yeah, I think I could use some Folake in my life.

95
00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.080
When you came to America,

96
00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:34.180
you played basketball, right?

97
00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:35.013
I did.

98
00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.260
I was in New York for the first 10 years

99
00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:40.140
and I went to the City College of New York,

100
00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:41.570
where I studied theater.

101
00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:45.380 line:15%
And for my last two years of college,

102
00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:47.670 line:15%
I got on the basketball team, Beavers.

103
00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:49.790 line:15%
And I had never played before.

104
00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:54.070 line:15%
I knew the rules from playing PlayStation with my brothers

105
00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:55.970
but the team was so desperate.

106
00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:57.765
I was able to get in,

107
00:03:57.765 --> 00:03:58.810
(laughs loudly)

108
00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:01.660
and I was a starter by the second year.

109
00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:03.810
You're also like into martial arts.

110
00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:06.910
Yeah, I always felt like I needed

111
00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:08.210
to prepare for the apocalypse.

112
00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:11.550
So I started taking Israeli martial arts.

113
00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:14.990
It's called Krav Maga when I moved to LA.

114
00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:16.310
And it's pretty badass.

115
00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:18.240
You know, it started to scare me for a while

116
00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.540
because it gave me a lot of confidence,

117
00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:24.153
but then the moves are so precise.

118
00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:27.810
They're so precise and lethal.

119
00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:31.040
So I started getting scared of actually getting in a fight.

120
00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:32.690
So I don't know if that's a good thing

121
00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:35.230
or a bad thing, but yeah.

122
00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:36.803
I studied Crab Louie.

123
00:04:38.848 --> 00:04:41.670
Oh, I did too Billy.

124
00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:44.360
I was really excited to talk to you about something

125
00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:49.360
which is that you are an awesome vet of stand-up comedy.

126
00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:52.580
Well, I don't know if I'm awesome

127
00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:54.670
but I did it for a long time.

128
00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:56.220
You really did.

129
00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:57.540
You made that rounds,

130
00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:00.340
you were traveling the country

131
00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:02.370
you know, 250 days out of the year.

132
00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:04.870
Tell me about those days.

133
00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:06.640
Well, I started in stand-up.

134
00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:09.220
You know, my dad was responsible for all that.

135
00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:11.350 line:15%
You know, he had Richard Pryor albums

136
00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:12.920 line:15%
and George Carlin albums

137
00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:14.220 line:15%
and Bob Newhart albums.

138
00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:16.970 line:15%
And he really introduced me to stand up

139
00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:19.640 line:15%
and I realized pretty quick

140
00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:21.430 line:15%
that I wasn't really going to college

141
00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:25.080 line:15%
and I wasn't really cut out for a lot.

142
00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:27.510 line:15%
So I loved the idea of words

143
00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:28.838
and making people laugh

144
00:05:28.838 --> 00:05:30.550
and that just clicked to me,

145
00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:31.400 line:15%
and I really loved it.

146
00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:33.610 line:15%
And then sitcoms were big with him too.

147
00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:34.443 line:15%
And when I was young,

148
00:05:34.443 --> 00:05:36.280 line:15%
we would watch "The Honeymooners" with him

149
00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:38.610
and I would watch "All in the Family"

150
00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:39.990
and "M*A*S*H" and "Cheers"

151
00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:42.000
and you know, "The Jeffersons."

152
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.870
And so I always dreamt about doing a sitcom

153
00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:48.880
and you know, I went out on the road at about 17

154
00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:50.540
and about 25 years later,

155
00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:52.013
I was an overnight success.

156
00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:59.090
Well, I started an online comedy club called Bananamore's.

157
00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:00.203
Really?

158
00:06:00.203 --> 00:06:01.220 line:15%
Yes, I did,

159
00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:04.790
because I felt like those rooms are dark

160
00:06:04.790 --> 00:06:06.010 line:15%
and empty right now,

161
00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:08.440 line:15%
and we need laughter,

162
00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:09.500 line:15%
we need comedy.

163
00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:10.950
More than ever.

164
00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:15.050
Can I ask you about a story that really jumped out at me

165
00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:18.040
that the day you met your wife,

166
00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:20.297
you saw her three times.

167
00:06:21.742 --> 00:06:22.575
I did.

168
00:06:22.575 --> 00:06:24.500
I saw her three times in the same day.

169
00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:26.060
I saw her at a bank in Atlanta,

170
00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:28.490
then I was performing at a comedy club that night

171
00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:31.910
and her and her sister just happened to be there.

172
00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:33.420
And I spotted her there.

173
00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:36.000
And then I went to a jazz club afterwards

174
00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:36.940
and she was there.

175
00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:39.810
And I've told this story a bunch of times

176
00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:42.000 line:15%
but I actually put it in my act.

177
00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:43.450 line:15%
When I saw her in the jazz club

178
00:06:43.450 --> 00:06:44.870 line:15%
I figured three times in one day

179
00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:45.770 line:15%
you gotta take a shot.

180
00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:47.850 line:15%
So I ordered a glass of wine.

181
00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:49.310 line:15%
I took her to her table and I said,

182
00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:51.870
here, drink this until I'm cute,

183
00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:54.240
and which I thought was a pretty good line

184
00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:55.540
until my wife looked at me and goes,

185
00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:57.110
I'm gonna need another one.

186
00:06:57.110 --> 00:06:58.500
I was like, I'm in love.

187
00:06:58.500 --> 00:06:59.793
That was the end of it.

188
00:06:59.793 --> 00:07:02.060
Man, that must really just mean something.

189
00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:04.550
You ran into her three times in one day.

190
00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:05.680
I mean that's.

191
00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:07.280
You know, she's been the best thing

192
00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:09.290
that ever happened to me.

193
00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:11.420
She's just stood by me all the way,

194
00:07:11.420 --> 00:07:12.550
she's been my rock,

195
00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:13.990
she's been my good sense,

196
00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:15.160
my logical brain.

197
00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:17.720
And I think it takes you a while

198
00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:20.410
when you're married to realize how smart your wife is.

199
00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:22.600
And you know, if I'm listening to her

200
00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:24.190
chances are I'm not in trouble.

201
00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:25.540
So I'm good with that.

202
00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:28.700
I tend to follow people who are way smarter, wiser

203
00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:31.440
and more Zen and emotionally capable than me,

204
00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:33.803
but at least I'm following better people.

205
00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:36.390
But I know that,

206
00:07:36.390 --> 00:07:38.060
you were just about to give up,

207
00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:39.700
you had been doing standup comedy

208
00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:41.986
and yet got the call for "Mike and Molly."

209
00:07:41.986 --> 00:07:42.819
Well, kind of.

210
00:07:42.819 --> 00:07:44.720
It had just kind of dried up.

211
00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:46.290
You know, I was in that period in LA,

212
00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:47.300 line:15%
that everybody goes through

213
00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:50.300 line:15%
where it was like just a year or two with no work,

214
00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:51.680 line:15%
nothing on the horizon.

215
00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:53.970
And I said, I'm going to hang out one more pilot season.

216
00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:56.720
And I only got six auditions in that pilot season

217
00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:58.890
and the last one was "Mike and Molly."

218
00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:01.320
It was literally a Hail Mary into the end zone,

219
00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:03.270
and then everything changed.

220
00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:05.280
I just happened to be standing in the right place

221
00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:06.693
at the right time twice.

222
00:08:07.605 --> 00:08:08.655
(laughs loudly)

223
00:08:08.655 --> 00:08:09.995
So I'm very blessed,

224
00:08:09.995 --> 00:08:10.828
very, very blessed

225
00:08:10.828 --> 00:08:12.750
Or maybe it was just all meant to be

226
00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:13.680
and you earned it.

227
00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:15.443
And you deserved it.
Well, maybe.

228
00:08:15.443 --> 00:08:16.276
I don't know about that.

229
00:08:16.276 --> 00:08:18.427
Well Billy and Folake,

230
00:08:18.427 --> 00:08:21.123
"Bob Hearts Abishola" airs Monday nights at 8:30

231
00:08:21.123 --> 00:08:25.653
7:30 Central on CBS and CBS All Access.

232
00:08:25.653 --> 00:08:28.236 line:15%
(upbeat music)

